  <li><strong>Daylight condition re‑inspection</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>Position the vehicle in a well‑lit area, preferably with natural sunlight, to reveal any surface imperfections.</li>
      <li>Compare the current appearance with the pickup photographs taken at the origin, checking for scratches, dents, paint chips, or missing accessories.</li>
      <li>Note any discrepancies on the handover checklist, photographing any new damage for record‑keeping.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li><strong>Odometer verification</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>Read the odometer reading displayed on the instrument cluster.</li>
      <li>Cross‑reference this reading with the mileage recorded on the LR (Logistics Receipt) bilty provided at pickup.</li>
      <li>Document any variance; a minor increase is expected due to transit, but significant differences must be investigated and justified.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li><strong>Signed condition sign‑off</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>Both the delivery agent and the recipient review the inspection findings together.</li>
      <li>The recipient signs the condition report, confirming acceptance of the vehicle’s state and mileage.</li>
      <li>The delivery agent also signs, acknowledging that the handover was completed according to protocol.</li>
      <li>A copy of the signed document is retained by the carrier, while the original is handed to the recipient for their records.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ul>

<p>By adhering to this three‑step handover—daylight visual re‑inspection, precise odometer verification against the LR bilty, and a mutually signed condition sign‑off—logistics providers ensure transparency, reduce disputes, and maintain the integrity of two‑wheeler and motorcycle deliveries from origin to destination.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>



